// Drives apt-daily.timer / apt-daily-upgrade.timer.
//
// Unattended-Upgrade is DISABLED ("0") and apt-daily-upgrade.timer is masked by
// scripts/install-host-config.sh — the host does not patch itself. This is a
// standing operator decision, not a temporary measure while something is debugged.
//
// Why: patching this box and rebooting it are one action, and that action needs a
// human who can reach the machine if it does not come back. Kernel and Docker
// updates only take effect on a reboot, and the operator is periodically away for
// weeks at a time with no access to the host. An unattended upgrade that lands
// during one of those stretches is a change nobody can verify, on a box nobody can
// recover, for however long the absence lasts. Deferring to a hands-on window
// trades a patch delay for the ability to fix what breaks.
//
// Consequence: Debian security updates are NOT applied automatically. Patching is
// a manual action, done when the operator can reach the machine:
//     sudo unattended-upgrade --dry-run    # preview under the 52homelab policy
//     sudo apt-get update && sudo apt-get upgrade
//     sudo reboot                          # if /var/run/reboot-required exists
//
// Update-Package-Lists stays "1" on purpose. apt-daily.timer only refreshes the
// package index and downloads — it installs nothing — and that refresh is what
// keeps `apt list --upgradable` accurate for scripts/cron-jobs/os-update-check.sh,
// which is now the only thing telling you security updates are pending. Turning it
// off would leave the box unpatched AND silent about it.
//
// The install policy in 52homelab-unattended-upgrades is retained so a manual
// `unattended-upgrade` run still applies the security-only, Docker- and
// kernel-blacklisted policy, and so re-enabling is a one-line change here plus
// unmasking the timer.
APT::Periodic::Update-Package-Lists "1";
APT::Periodic::Unattended-Upgrade "0";
APT::Periodic::AutocleanInterval "7";
